Windows Registry Editor Version 5.00

; YAZX New Tweaks — CANDIDATE & EXPERIMENTAL Registry Settings
; STATUS: NOT for auto-import. Each tweak must be evaluated individually.
; DO NOT apply to current system.
;
; HOW TO USE:
;   1. Review each tweak below
;   2. Import individually (copy single sections to .reg files)
;   3. Benchmark before/after each
;   4. Revert if no measurable improvement

; =====================================================================
; CANDIDATE: GlobalTimerResolutionRequests [CANDIDATE]
; =====================================================================
; WHAT: Allows the OS to honor the highest timer resolution requested
;        by any running process, system-wide.
; WHY: Some games and media apps request high-res timers (0.5ms).
;       Without this, Windows 11 24H2 may ignore those requests to
;       save power.
; NOTE: This is a SERVICE-LEVEL setting. It does NOT set the timer
;       resolution itself — it allows applications and drivers that
;       request higher resolution to get it. The actual timer resolution
;       is still determined by running processes (e.g., game engines,
;       multimedia timers, NtSetTimerResolution calls).
; COMPATIBILITY: Windows 11 24H2 (Build 26100). Present since 22H2.
; RISK: Low. May increase idle power by ~1W if a background process
;       holds a high-res timer request. No stability risk.
; RESTORE: Set to 0 or delete the value.
; SOURCE: Microsoft documentation on timer resolution changes in Win11.
;
; [HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager\kernel]
; "GlobalTimerResolutionRequests"=dword:00000001

; =====================================================================
; CANDIDATE: LargeSystemCache = 0 [CANDIDATE]
; =====================================================================
; WHAT: Tells the memory manager to prioritize application working sets
;       over filesystem cache.
; WHY: With 32GB RAM, the system has ample memory. Setting this to 0
;       ensures games get priority over file caching.
; NOTE: Windows default on workstation SKUs is already 0. This may
;       already be the current value. Verify with:
;       (Get-ItemProperty "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CCS\Control\Session Manager\Memory Management").LargeSystemCache
; CURRENT VALUE: 0 (default on workstation — this confirms the default)
; COMPATIBILITY: All Windows versions.
; RISK: Minimal. If the value is already 0, this changes nothing.
; RESTORE: Set to 0 (which is the default anyway).
;
; [HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager\Memory Management]
; "LargeSystemCache"=dword:00000000

; =====================================================================
; EXPERIMENTAL: Disable Memory Compression [EXPERIMENTAL]
; =====================================================================
; WHAT: Disables Windows memory page compression/combining.
; WHY: Reduces CPU overhead from compressing/decompressing memory pages.
; NOTE: This is NOT a registry setting. It requires PowerShell:
;       Disable-MMAgent -MemoryCompression
; CURRENT STATE: Unknown (Get-MMAgent requires elevation, blocked pre-format)
; COMPATIBILITY: Windows 10 1607+.
; RISK: MEDIUM. With 32GB RAM the risk is low, but:
;       - If a game or application causes high memory pressure, the
;         system will page to disk instead of compressing, which is SLOWER
;       - There is no published benchmark showing consistent gaming benefit
;       - Microsoft enables it by default for a reason
; RESTORE: Enable-MMAgent -MemoryCompression
; VERDICT: Do NOT auto-apply. Test manually post-format and benchmark.
;
; (No registry key — PowerShell command only)
